ch diagonal at most one point. The maximum number of points a sunny line can cover is at most n (since there are n columns). But can it achieve n? Let's see if there exists a line not parallel to any of the three families that passes through n points of the set. That would require a set of n points with distinct x, distinct y, distinct x+y. This is a set of points forming a permutation matrix with distinct sums. This is known as a \"Sidon set\"? Actually it's a set of points (i, π(i)) where π is a permutation and i+π(i) are all distinct. For i from 1 to n, i+π(i) are distinct if and only if π(i) ≠ π(j) for i≠j (true) and i+π(i) ≠ j+π(j). This is a condition that the sum of index and value is distinct. For a permutation, i+π(i) can be equal for different i if π(i) = π(j) + (j-i). Since π is a permutation, it's possible to have collisions. For example, for n=3, permutation (1,2,3) gives sums (2,4,6) distinct. Permutation (2,1,3) gives sums (3,3,6) not distinct. So some permutations have distinct sums. So we can find permutations where sums are distinct. For n=3, (1,2,3) works. For n=4, (1,2,3,4) gives sums (2,4,6,8) distinct. So identity permutation works. But does identity permutation satisfy i+π(i) ≤ n+1? For i=1, π(1)=1 => sum=2 ≤ n+1=5 (for n=4). For i=2, sum=4 ≤5; i=3 sum=6 >5, fails. So identity fails for i> floor((n+1)/2). So we need a permutation where i+π(i) ≤ n+1 for all i. The reverse permutation π(i)=n+1-i yields sum = n+1 for all i, which is allowed (equal to n+1). So that works. But that set of points lies on the diagonal x+y=n+1, which is not sunny. So any line passing through those points would be parallel to x+y=0, thus not sunny. So a sunny line cannot cover n points. So the maximum number of points a sunny line can cover is less than n. Let's find the maximum possible. Since each sunny line cannot be parallel to any of the three families, it can intersect each column at most one point, each row at most one point, each diagonal at most one point. So 