ertical x=3 covers column 3: (3,1)-(3,4). (3,4) also covered by diagonal.\n\nDiagonal x+y=7 covers points (4,3),(5,2),(6,1) (plus points already covered). So after using these lines, uncovered points are:\n\nColumn 4: points (4,1),(4,2) (since (4,3) covered by diagonal). Column 5: point (5,1) (since (5,2) covered). Column 6: none (since (6,1) covered). So uncovered points: (4,1),(4,2),(5,1). That's 3 points. We have 2 sunny lines left. Points: (4,1) row1 sum5, (4,2) row2 sum6, (5,1) row1 sum6. Row1 appears twice: (4,1) and (5,1). So they cannot be on same sunny line. So we need at least 2 sunny lines for row1 points, which we have. Then (4,2) can be assigned to either sunny line, as long as constraints hold.\n\nLet's try assign:\n\nSunny line S1: (4,1) (col4 row1 sum5) and (4,2) (col4 row2 sum6) cannot be same line because same column. So S1 can only have one of them.\n\nSunny line S2: (5,1) (col5 row1 sum6) and maybe (4,2) (col4 row2 sum6) cannot be same line because sum6 conflict (both sum6). So S2 cannot have both (5,1) and (4,2). So we need to assign (4,2) to a line that does not have row2 conflict (none) and sum6 conflict (if line has sum6 already). S1 currently has (4,1) sum5, row1. So S1 can take (4,2) because sum6 distinct, row2 distinct, column 4 conflict? Actually S1 already has a point in column 4 (4,1). So cannot have another point in column 4. So S1 cannot take (4,2). So (4,2) cannot be assigned to either S1 or S2 because S1 already has column 4 point, S2 has column 5 point, but S2 could take (4,2) because column 4 is different from column 5, row2 is distinct from row1, sum6 is distinct from sum6? Wait S2 has (5,1) sum6, so sum6 conflict. So S2 cannot take (4,2). So (4,2) cannot be assigned to any sunny line. So this configuration fails.\n\nThus we need a different arrangement of non-sunny lines.\n\nMaybe we can use horizontal lines instead of vertical lines for some columns. Let's try using horizontal lines y=1, y=2, y=3, and a diagonal line x+y=7. That's 4 non-sunny lines. Then we have 2 sunny lines left.\n\nHorizontal y=1 covers (1,1)-(6,1). Horizontal y=2 covers (1,2)-(5,2). Horizontal y=3 covers (1,3)-(4,3). Diagonal x+y=7 covers (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1). Let's see uncovered points:\n\nPoints covered by horizontals:\n\n- y=1: (1,1),(2,1),(3,1),(4,1),(5,1),(6,1)\n- y=2: (1,2),(2,2),(3,2),(4,2),(5,2)\n- y=3: (1,3),(2,3),(3,3),(4,3)\n\nDiagonal x+y=7 covers (1,6),(2,5),(3,4),(4,3),(5,2),(6,1). Some of these are already covered by horizontals.\n\nNow list all points (a,b) with a+b ≤7:\n\nColumn 1: (1,1)-(1,6)\nColumn 2: (2,1)-(2,5)\nColumn 3: (3,1)-(3,4)\nColumn 4: (4,1)-(4,3)\nColumn 5: (5,1)-(5,2)\nColumn 6: (6,1)\n\nNow mark covered:\n\n- (1,1)-(1,3) covered by horizontals y=1,2,3. (1,4) not covered (since y=4 not covered). (1,5) not covered. (1,6) covered by diagonal.\n\n- (2,1)-(2,3) covered by horizontals. (2,4) not covered. (2,5) covered by diagonal.\n\n- (3,1)-(3,3) covered by horizontals. (3,4) covered by diagonal.\n\n- (4,1)-(4,3) covered by horizontals (y=1,2,3). Actually (4,3) also covered by diagonal, but that's fine.\n\n- (5,1)-(5,2) covered by horizontals (y=1,2). (5,2) also covered by diagonal.\n\n- (6,1) covered by horizontals (y=1) and diagonal.\n\nThus uncovered points are: (1,4),(1,5), (2,4), (2,? maybe (2,? ) Actually (2,4) uncovered, (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) Let's list all points not covered:\n\n- (1,4) sum5, row4\n- (1,5) sum6, row5\n- (2,4) sum6, row4\n- (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) Actually (2,5) covered by diagonal, (2,4) uncovered, (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) (2,? ) So only (2,4) uncovered.\n\n- (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) (3,? ) Actually (3,4) covered by diagonal, (3,? ) all others covered.\n\n- (4,? ) all covered.\n\n- (5,? ) all covered.\n\n- (6,? ) all covered.\n\nThus uncovered points: (1,4),(1,5),(2,4). That's 3 points. We have 2 sunny lines left. Points: (1,4) row4 sum5, (1,5) row5 sum6, (2,4) row4 sum6. Row4 appears twice: (1,4) and (2,4)